humanize/references/signs-of-ai-writing.md is a condensed and rewritten
derivative of that page. It preserves the page's five-part structure
(Language and tone; Style; Markup; Citations; Communication intended for the
user), its specific pattern lists, and its governing caveats. The wording is
substantially rewritten; the taxonomy and examples are not original to this
repository.

A NOTE ON HOW IT WAS BUILT
--------------------------
en.wikipedia.org was cache-only and could not be fetched directly in the
environment where this skill was assembled. The taxonomy was therefore
reconstructed from detailed secondary coverage of the page rather than from
the page itself.

This is disclosed because it matters: the reconstruction is faithful as far as
could be verified, but it has not been diffed line by line against the live
page. Users are encouraged to check it and open pull requests for anything
that has drifted or was missed.

Other sources referenced
------------------------
- Liang et al. (Stanford), "GPT detectors are biased against non-native
  English writers" (2023), for the 61.3% misclassification figure.
- Published analyses of sentence-length distribution ("burstiness") in
  human- vs AI-flagged text, for the short-sentence proportions cited in
